# What `gcloud builds submit` uploads as the build context.
#
# Without this file gcloud falls back to .gitignore, which does NOT exclude
# public/ — so every build would upload the full 64 MB regulatory corpus to reach
# the one 14 MB file the image actually needs.
#
# Keep in step with .dockerignore: this decides what leaves the machine, that
# decides what the Docker daemon sees. Anything dropped here can never be COPYed,
# so the rag-chunks re-include below is load-bearing.
#
# IMPORTANT — this file does NOT share .dockerignore's matching rules, and the
# difference has bitten this build before:
#
#   * .dockerignore anchors every pattern at the context root, so a bare
#     `src` there means only the SPA's `/src`.
#   * .gcloudignore uses .gitignore syntax (`gcloud topic gcloudignore`), where a
#     pattern with NO slash matches at ANY depth — so a bare `src` here would
#     also swallow `server/src`, and `server/Dockerfile`'s `COPY server/src`
#     would fail with "file not found in build context".
#
# Hence the leading `/` on every root-only entry below. Do not remove them, and
# do not "simplify" this file to match .dockerignore's shorter forms.

.git
.gitignore
node_modules
*/node_modules
*.log

# The deploy workflow writes .env.local before building; it has no business
# in a Cloud Build source tarball even when it holds only publishable keys.
.env
.env.*

/server/lib
/server/tests
/server/coverage

/dist
/dev-dist
/coverage
/test-results
/playwright-report
/.github
/.claude
/android
/ios
/apple
/archive
/e2e
/src
/tests
/screenshots
/supabase
/worker

# public/ is 64 MB and the image needs exactly one file from it.
#
# gitignore semantics cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is
# excluded — gcloud prunes an excluded directory from its walk, so a bare
# `public` + `!public/data/rag-chunks.json` silently drops the corpus and the
# image build fails on COPY. Each level has to be re-included on the way down.
/public/*
!/public/data/
/public/data/*
!/public/data/rag-chunks.json
